      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "Recessive mutations in the anoctamin-5 gene (ANO5) cause a spectrum of clinical phenotypes, including limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (LGMD 2L), distal myopathy, and asymptomatic hyperCKemia."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "In this report we describe our clinical, electrophysiological, pathological, and molecular findings in a subject with anoctaminopathy-5."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "A 49-year-old Arabic man from a consanguineous family presented with a 5-year history of myalgias, hyperCKemia and an episode of unprovoked rhabdomyolysis. Muscle biopsy showed mild myopathic changes and interstitial amyloid deposition. ANO5 analysis detected a novel homozygous deletion of approximately 11.9 kb encompassing exons 13-17, predicted to be pathogenic."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Anoctaminopathy-5 can manifest with a phenotype reminiscent of metabolic myopathy and should be considered as a potential cause of myalgia and myoglobinuria. Amyloid deposition in the muscle biopsy is helpful for the diagnosis. A novel homozygous ANO5 deletion was identified, suggesting that screening for common mutations may have low yield in non-European subjects."
          }
        ]
      },
